WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:03.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:05.800
D. FOX HARRELL: The MIT Center
for Advanced Virtuality,

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.680
you could look at it
as a way to investigate

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.600
how we can blend the imagination
with the physical world.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.570
A lot of our work involves
simulating social phenomena,

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.150
such as discrimination
in the workplace,

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.830
bias within the classrooms.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.750
How we see each other in
different circumstances,

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.300
depending on our background.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.180
Ways that we are categorized.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.390
How can we use these simulations
to spark the imagination,

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.210
to spark prospective
transformation?

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.030
We have done work on issues
of everyday discrimination,

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.860
sometimes called
"microaggression,"

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.010
using video gaming as a
platform for addressing

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.450
this kind of issue.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.600
Our work also addresses
issues like personalization.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.580
Sometimes people
might have a sense

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.340
of needing to be pushed
in a certain kind of area

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.550
to be challenged.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.360
Sometimes people need to be
scaffolded and supported.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.360
And to have technologies
that can do either as needed,

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.450
I think is a kind
of crucial advance.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.480
ALAN SPOON: The future of
rich, educational experience

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.175
is interactive communications
in a post-pandemic world

00:01:09.175 --> 00:01:10.800
where remoteness is
evermore important,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.060
and where the technologies to
support that are multiplying.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:17.540
This center on an
integrated basis can--

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.010
with students and faculty and
tapping all corners of MIT--

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:25.910
be a critically important
significant contributor

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:27.105
to that thinking.

00:01:27.105 --> 00:01:29.480
D. FOX HARRELL: We are gratified
to receive an Emmy award

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.550
for interactive documentary.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:35.210
This particular work is called,
In Event of Moon Disaster,

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.520
and it's a project
that constructs

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.820
a deepfake of Richard Nixon
giving the speech that he

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:44.270
would have given if the moon
landing had been unsuccessful.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.760
RICHARD NIXON: Fate has
ordained that the men who

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.790
went to the moon to
explore in peace--

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:52.430
D. FOX HARRELL: It's actually
a beautiful and elegiac speech,

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:53.990
but it was never given.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:57.500
It's computer generated, using
machine learning techniques--

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.815
RICHARD NIXON: --to explore in
peace will stay on the moon.

00:02:00.815 --> 00:02:02.190
D. FOX HARRELL:
This is a project

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:05.210
which helps people to
understand, how do we recognize

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:06.530
this kind of technology?

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:09.860
Can we recognize this
kind of technology?

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.880
It's really a form of learning
that you could say responds

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.600
to the particular culture, not
just of the subject matter,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.210
but of the user of
the system as well.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.330
And I think that's
just a hallmark

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.490
of a kind of broader
approach in learning,

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:29.300
in which we can personalize
and customize experiences.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.520
There is not just one truth.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.030
And by seeing the same story
through different lenses,

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.240
that broader message can emerge.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.900
Helping to build this
studio and lab that

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.690
uses simulation and
technologies of virtual reality

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.030
for the social
good, and inventing

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:51.830
new forms of learning that's
engaging and immersive,

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:54.890
I would say that that's
the key towards pushing

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:56.920
our agenda forward.

